Say each BSS Transition Management request over D-Bus.

An access point asks a station to move with an 802.11v BSS Transition
Management request, naming candidate BSSes with a preference each, and
can warn that it will disassociate the station after a timer. Upstream
answers the request itself and tells a program on the bus only the
status it answered with, as the BSSTMStatus property. The candidates go
to the debug log alone, and the warning to the control socket.

A program choosing access points itself, holding a card to one BSSID,
then can't follow a warned request: with a fixed BSSID set, upstream
rejects any request whose candidates it didn't hear in its last scan,
and the access point drops the station when the timer runs out.

This adds a signal on the Interface, BSSTransitionRequested, sent as
each request is parsed and before it's answered, with one dictionary,
since a signal's table holds three arguments at most: Disassociating
(b), whether it warns of a disassociation from the BSS or the whole
ESS; DisassociationMs (u), in how long; ValidMs (u), how long its
candidate list holds; and Candidates (a(ayn)), each as its BSSID and its
preference, or -1 where the access point gave none, in the order given.
How upstream answers the request is left as it was.
